stage3/trunc - Stage 3 TRUNCATION test of the PSC decoder
=========================================================

WORKFLOW.md Stage 3 "Truncation": cut a dump at arbitrary byte offsets, as a
battery pull would; the decoder recovers every complete record before the
cut. DESIGN.md 8.5 asks for "truncation at every byte offset"; DESIGN.md 10.2
defines the parsing ("drop a truncated tail chunk, keep every earlier one";
4.5 "a chunk cut by the pull fails its CRC and is dropped").

Nothing frozen is touched. The decoder (work/decoder), the collector source
(work/pscol/pscol.c) and its host harness (work/pscol/test/sim.c, vectors.c)
are only read: gen_dumps.c #includes sim.c with its main() renamed and is
compiled into the scratch directory; trunc_test.py imports the decoder
modules and calls them. The only interposition is a spy on
pscdec_parse.load (same call, same return value, the collection is kept so
the parse layer is not run twice per cut).

Files
-----
gen_dumps.c       dump generator: the released pscol.c (unmodified) driven by
                  the pscol host harness sim.c (simulated /proc/psc, vfat,
                  Memory Stick at 52 KB/s); writes PSCLOG/T<rrr><nnn>.BIN (the
                  on-stick bytes), regions.tsv (the writer's own write()
                  regions: FILEHDR / FLUSH / STEP) and truth.txt
oracle.py         ground truth, independent of the decoder (written from
                  DESIGN 10.2/10.3; imports no decoder module): walks every
                  file strictly in writer order, verifies every CRC, gives the
                  chunk and record ledger with byte ranges and the pre-image
                  (the preallocation PAD sectors, DESIGN 4.4 step 3)
trunc_test.py     the test: gen | plan | run | summary
probe_phantom.py  targeted probe of a torn chunk header over a PAD sector
dumps/            the six dumps (SHA256SUMS), regenerated by "gen"
plan.json.gz      the cut sets actually run (from "plan"; deterministic)
REPORT.md         results

Dumps (trunc_test.py DUMP_SPECS)
--------------------------------
pscol-short    pscol, worker 5 s, end 16 s: 1 file in creation (247,296 B,
               62,976 written), 15 flushes; FILEHDR with its 768-byte stats
               block, UHB, STATS, KMSG, EVENT
pscol-resend   pscol, 5..20 s, the 6th flush's data writes fail: a FAILED
               flush region (PAD sectors) and a re-send block later
synth-short    decoder synth.py H2, 26 s: independent writer model, EPC-valid
               synthetic build, PROCS
pscol-wrapped  pscol, worker starts at 130 s: P and POLL rings lapped before
               the first read (first blocks lost 506 / 253), 40 KB catch-up
               flushes
pscol-span     pscol, 30..404 s: T001001 complete (2 MB), T001002 active (10
               flushes), T001003 in creation; records span the boundary
synth-H4       synth.py H4: 3 x 2 MB, 949 flushes, onset in the data

Models (what follows the cut)
-----------------------------
TRUNC    the file ends at the cut; later files of the run absent
OVERLAY  the file keeps its size; bytes after the cut are the pre-image (the
         preallocation PAD sectors the flush was overwriting); later files
         reverted to FILEHDR + PAD. This is what a pull leaves in a
         preallocated segment. A cut in file k >= 1 leaves the newer segment
         partially written beside the older complete one(s).
SUBSET   one tick flush torn with an arbitrary subset of its 512-byte
         sectors written (write-back order not assumed ascending)

Cut sets (plan)
---------------
"all" dumps (pscol-short, pscol-resend, synth-short):
   tier P at EVERY byte offset 0..size; tier F (whole program) at every byte
   offset 0..written extent + 512, plus in the PAD-sector region above it
   +-64 around the first 5 sector boundaries and the last, +-2 around every
   sector boundary, 500 random offsets.
"large" dumps (pscol-wrapped, pscol-span, synth-H4):
   tier P at every offset within 64 bytes of each chunk and record boundary
   of the focus flushes (first, last two, two random, every flush with a
   lapped or re-send block, the onset flush; a segment the writer switched
   from: its last three + two random), every byte of the FILEHDR area and of
   a partially written segment with <= 64 KB written (pscol-span T001002),
   each chunk start -1..+1 in every other flush, +-64 around the first PAD
   sectors and the last; plus 500 random offsets per dump (seed 20261007),
   both tiers. Tier F at every chunk boundary -1..+1 and magic byte in the
   FILEHDR area and focus flushes, record start/end-1/end in the first two
   focus RECS chunks (all of them in pscol-span T001002), the 500 random.
SUBSET: every subset for a flush of <= 10 sectors, else 256 random subsets
   plus none and all; the last flush of every file (large dumps: first,
   middle, last).

Checks per cut (trunc_test.py check_parse, check_program)
---------------------------------------------------------
(a) no exception from the decoder (SystemExit included), exit status in
    {0, 2, 3} and consistent with the collection (2 refused, 3 raw image
    required, else 0)
(b) every record of every chunk that is complete in the cut dump is merged
    with the same bytes as the oracle and as the uncut decode (tier F: the
    same CSV row in p/poll/w/s/m.csv); nothing else is merged; every
    complete UHB, STATS, EVENT, KMSG, PROCS chunk and FILEHDR is kept; tier F
    stats.csv / uhb.csv / events.txt rows are rows of the uncut decode.
    Separately ("listed"): each record wholly before the cut inside the cut
    chunk appears in bad.txt as UNVERIFIED with the same bytes.
(c) the decoder's statement about the cut file: explicit (a chunk BAD
    truncated / truncated-header / bad-crc in that file), refused, size (RAW
    IMAGE REQUIRED naming the cut size; TRUNC only; "size-also-uncut" when
    the uncut file already had that reason), anomaly (only a new REPORT
    anomaly line), none. N/A when nothing the writer wrote is lost: TRUNC
    only the uncut file; OVERLAY/SUBSET when the file equals an untorn
    writer state or only flush padding was torn ("pad-only").
Phantoms: chunks accepted in the cut file that the writer never wrote
    there (other than pre-image PAD sectors) are counted.

Run
---
  export TRUNC_SCRATCH=<scratch dir>      (gen_dumps binary, plan, refs, summaries)
  python3 trunc_test.py gen               (logs/trunc-gen.log, dumps/SHA256SUMS)
  python3 trunc_test.py plan
  python3 trunc_test.py run --jobs 16     (logs/trunc-<dump>.tsv.gz, one row per cut)
  python3 trunc_test.py summary
  python3 probe_phantom.py                (logs/trunc-probe-phantom.log)
Per-cut working files go to /dev/shm/trunc-<pid>/ (decoder inputs and outputs).
Host: Python 3.12, gcc 13 (64-bit; the -m32 fallback of run.sh).
